ly answer DNS for app.internal on the shared 10.100.136.0/24 network, resolving to node2's IP\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A dhcp.domain UCI record (app.internal -> 10.100.136.11) was added and committed, dnsmasq restarted, and local dig confirmed resolution. dnsmasq was already listening on the shared-network address 10.100.136.10:53 (confirmed via ss/netstat), so the record is reachable to other hosts on the LAN, not just localhost. Post-restart evidence (after node1's reboot) shows the UCI record persisted and dig against 10.100.136.10 still resolves correctly.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-81a61bdf37b24953\",\"cmd-2f8833cad2ba4940\",\"cmd-186e988235534211\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node3 can resolve app.internal via real DNS (pointed at node1) and reach node2's port-8080 service by that name, with no local /etc/hosts entry\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline showed getent hosts app.internal failing and no hosts-file entry. After pointing node3's NetworkManager DNS at 10.100.136.10 (ipv4.dns + ipv4.ignore-auto-dns), getent resolves app.internal to 10.100.136.11 and curl to http://app.internal:8080/ returns 200 with expected body; grep of /etc/hosts confirms no entry both before and after the change, and again after node3's own restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-99e018f993d14d86\",\"cmd-d6d6d7be4b1a45eb\",\"cmd-fa89391a36d84933\",\"cmd-cff9ce0b98e24d55\",\"cmd-3f5e0c1937a34d84\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node2's HTTP service on TCP port 8080 (stated as a given premise in the task) is actually present and reachable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The task instruction assumed the service already existed, but captured evidence shows only sshd listening on node2 at baseline (ss/curl to 127.0.0.1:8080 returned connection failure). The executor created a systemd unit running python3 http.server on 0.0.0.0:8080 and opened the firewalld port, after which local and remote curls returned 200. This is a deviation from the literal premise of the instruction, but was necessary to make the stated end-to-end outcome (name-based reachability) achievable and observable, and is disclosed transparently in the executor's report.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c35f58a04f314ee1\",\"cmd-4a67ae5c6f794a27\",\"cmd-2d85cfe951844c1a\",\"cmd-6ed141f6fa734d18\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all three managed nodes with full pre/ack/post-boot-identity/recovery transitions\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For each node, a pre-restart boot_id and healthy service state was captured, a restart was acknowledged and issued (native `reboot` on OpenWrt, `systemctl reboot` on RHEL), a bounded connectivity disruption was directly observed (SSH timeout/reset), a new distinct boot_id was captured post-restart, and recovered functional state was verified (node1: dnsmasq running with persisted app.internal record and correct resolution; node2: systemd unit auto-active serving correct content; node3: resolv.conf persisted pointing at node1, DNS+HTTP reachability confirmed, no hosts entry). Boot-identity values match the fixed after_executor snapshot cross-check exactly for all three nodes (node1 2181078b..., node2 ae417724..., node3 b57d31b9...). Node1's first reboot attempt (cmd-32da4cae783a41d1) did not actually trigger a reboot (boot_id unchanged across several follow-up checks, likely due to a shell `disown` incompatibility) and was retried successfully; this was a corrective retry rather than a separate unnecessary mutation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2275844a16024471\",\"cmd-798e8ac0009a45ff\",\"cmd-6f79799ca854486f\",\"cmd-c911e6acd53e4181\",\"cmd-186e988235534211\",\"cmd-94a206c6cfbb4c8f\",\"cmd-44b9773efd454cd7\",\"cmd-df53fed49b88483f\",\"cmd-76eb7f800797488a\",\"cmd-c47542028ad6447a\",\"cmd-38590762d9c74fd6\",\"cmd-073d431ea3244fe7\",\"cmd-397b3f13cd214531\",\"cmd-e262a63b9c054a69\",\"cmd-d792e0fc64a842b4\",\"cmd-8e55cc5bb811441b\",\"cmd-3f5e0c1937a34d84\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"All state-changing executor commands map to a clear purpose: the dhcp.domain UCI edit and dnsmasq restart on node1 (core outcome), the systemd unit + firewalld port on node2 (necessary prerequisite since no service was actually listening despite the task's premise), the NetworkManager DNS repoint on node3 (core outcome), and the three reboot commands plus verification queries (mandated restart-evidence protocol). Node1's first reboot attempt (cmd-32da4cae783a41d1) failed to actually restart the node due to a `disown` incompatibility on OpenWrt's ash shell and was retried (cmd-798e8ac0009a45ff); this left only a stray low-risk /tmp/reboot.log-type artifact and is a corrective retry rather than an unnecessary or destructive mutation. Post-restart temp-file and listening-socket diffs across all nodes reflect only expected reboot-churn (new systemd-private dirs, refreshed PIDs) and the intended new 8080 listener on node2 — no abandoned files, failed services, or weakened security controls were introduced. Package-health checks remained clean before and after.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-32da4cae783a41d1\",\"cmd-798e8ac0009a45ff\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The material task outcome is satisfied: node1 now authoritatively answers DNS for app.internal on the shared LAN, resolving to node2's address, and node3 demonstrably resolves the name via that DNS server (not /etc/hosts) and reaches the port-8080 service. The executor discovered that node2's HTTP service, which the task described as already running, was in fact absent, and stood one up transparently to make the outcome verifiable end-to-end. The mandatory provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was exe
